{
  "abstract": "We prove a fully explicit zero-density estimate with Huxley's exponent on a short range. For $\\sigma\\in[0.755,0.785]$ and $T\\ge T_1$, where $T_1=10^{175000}$ for $\\sigma<0.765$ and $T_1=10^{160000}$ otherwise, the number of zeros of $\\zeta$ with $\\beta\\ge\\sigma$ and $T_1<\\gamma\\le T$ is at most $10^{188}\\,T^{3(1-\\sigma)/(3\\sigma-1)}(\\log T)^{45}$. A corollary bounds $N(\\sigma,T)$ for $\\log_{10}T\\ge182721$. The proof uses Montgomery's mollified detector, a smooth dyadic block lemma that removes the zero-dependent weight at constant cost, an explicit form of Huxley's large-values estimate, explicit coefficient mean squares, and a case analysis over four exponent variables. An appendix derives the finite inequalities that place every detected zero in the certified domain. A box decomposition, re-checked in exact rational arithmetic, verifies the case analysis. The main analytic constants and the comparison behind the corollary are ball-arithmetic enclosures; the remaining constants are floating-point evaluations with stated margins. The log power is tracked term by term, and each used term has nonpositive excess (in the moving corner near $a=1/2$, nonpositive base excess), so the bound at $T_1$ holds for all larger $T$. We then compare the result with the explicit applications we examined, and it improves none of them. Its leading term beats the explicit Ingham-shape bound of Chourasiya and Simonič only above $10^{6645}$ to $10^{42700}$; these crossover heights lie below the threshold $T_1$ from which Theorem 1 holds. At the heights used by a conditional prime-gap threshold ($T\\approx10^{210}$), the whole Huxley–Ingham exponent gap is worth $0.7$ to $4.3$ powers of $\\log T$. At the Korobov–Vinogradov edge, in a model of Cully-Hugill's argument, Bellotti's near-unity bound beats every Huxley-shaped bound whose log power exceeds about $6.2$. This quantifies, for these applications, Bellotti's observation that log powers can outweigh exponent gains. We make no claim about applications or implementations outside those analysed.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Revision of riemann_hypothesis_130 v1 addressing the minor corrections in the acceptance review. Theorem 1, Corollary 2, T1, the constant 10^188 and the log power 45 are unchanged.\n(1) Certification labels. cpsi_rig.py was mislabelled exact; it is floating point. Lemma 4.1 is now proved by cpsi_arb.py in Arb ball arithmetic (SymPy rational root isolation, exact antiderivatives, Hurwitz-zeta tail for |k| > 2000): Sigma_Psi in [14.9482152173 +/- 1.5e-13] < 14.95; cpsi_rig.py is kept and labelled float, superseded. The mpmath upper bound r0_up behind eta_up in certify_exact3.py is now stated in the text and README and checked against an Arb enclosure by eta_arb.py (all six slices, margin > 3e-35). The status line and abstract now say which constants are Arb and which are float (Lemma 3.2, the v-tail of Lemma 3.3, Lemma 7.1, Section 8, Appendix A).\n(2) Corner monotonicity. Section 1 and Section 6.1 now separate fixed boxes (used terms have excess <= 0) from the moving corner a = 1/2 + u eta (P = p + e0/eta + u e1, condition e0 <= 0 on the base excess only; the full excess may be positive). Heading 4.3 is now \"Three options at two witness points\" and names t_A and t_F.\n(3) Numbers. Last-slice log10 B_I 44.2 -> 44.3 (bcalc.out); A_2 <= 8.052 -> 8.0521 (enclosure 8.0520207); Lemma 4.1 now defines F(u) = log 2 * Psi(2^u) via x = 2^u, so the Jacobian is explicit in the derivative integrals.\n(4) Abstract: the crossover heights against Chourasiya-Simonic are leading-term comparisons and lie below T1.\n(5) Found in our own re-audit. certify_exact3.py and cover3.py now use the exact outer edges of each region (sigma_0, delta = 3/25, U0, 0.505, a_lo) instead of the nearest floats, which differ by up to 1e-14. One box then needs a face check at s1 = 0, done with its recorded option; all slices still PASS with the same maxima. The elementary Mertens-product bound behind Lemma 5.3 is now written out, and its constant log 4 + C <= 2.1417 is checked in Arb (mertens_arb.py). README labels are now checked mechanically against the scripts before submission; stale 'rigorous' strings in two superseded float/evidence scripts were removed.",
